      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through the exchange around Sasha's Apology line by line and unpack how the Russian works.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Я не хотела, правда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I didn't mean to, honestly.' Sasha chooses the imperfective verb meaning 'wanted' for the ongoing action, followed by 'honestly'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Я сейчас все вытру.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will wipe it all up now.' Here Sasha anchors the line on the word meaning 'now' and builds the thought around 'to wipe'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не надо плакать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Don't cry.' The phrasing opens with 'it is necessary' before bringing in 'to cry' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Все хорошо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Everything is fine.' Max delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'good'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Вы меня уволите?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Will you fire me?' Sasha uses the polite plural 'you' alongside 'will fire', keeping a formal or respectful register.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Вы расскажете все руководству.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You will tell management everything.' Sasha uses the perfective verb meaning 'will tell' to focus on the single completed result, pairing it with 'leadership'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Никуда я не пойду.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am not going anywhere.' The phrasing opens with 'nowhere' before bringing in 'go' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ничего я не скажу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I won't say anything.' Max delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'I'll tell'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Извините меня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Excuse me.' Sasha uses the imperative command form of the verb meaning 'sorry'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Я правда не хотела.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I really didn't mean to.' Here Sasha anchors the line on the word meaning 'honestly' and builds the thought around 'wanted'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Успокойся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Calm down.' Max replies with a single verb form meaning 'calm down' — in Russian, the verb ending alone carries the subject and tense.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Иди работать дальше.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Go on working.' Max leads with the direct command form meaning 'go', pairing it with 'to work' and 'next'. Now let's listen to the whole conversation again from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
